{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "284",
    "case_summary": "A 62-year-old nonsmoking man presented with multiple pleural thickenings and a marked effusion on the left side. The patient underwent first-line chemotherapy followed by left pleurectomy, with postsurgical assessment revealing residual microscopic disease. External beam radiotherapy was administered. The disease remained stable until July 2023, when a nodular growth appeared on the mid third of the tongue.\n\nNodular growth observed on the mid third of the tongue.\n\n- Imaging Studies:    Initial CT scan showed multiple pleural thickenings and marked effusion on the left side.      - Pathological Examinations:    1. Initial Video-assisted thoracoscopy (March 2022):    - Epithelioid solid formations with pseudopapillary structures    - Immunoreactive for calretinin, AE1/AE3, D2-40, Wilms tumor protein 1    - Absence of nuclear staining for BRCA1-associated protein 1    - Negative for methylthioadenosine phosphorylase    - Homozygous deletion of CDKN2A gene by fluorescent in-situ hybridization        2. Tongue nodule examination:    Figure 1. Title: Microscopic Assessment of Tongue Metastasis    Description: Neoplastic subepithelial proliferation, consisting of epithelioid cells in the tongue (hematoxylin-eosin stain; original magnification ×50)        Figure 2. Title: Microscopic Findings    Description: Neoplastic cells immunoreactive for calretinin, confirming mesothelial origin (peroxidase stain; original magnification ×100)",
